🔑 Key Points
We used merge(key, value, remappingFunction) → which ensures atomic updates.
Both threads safely incremented the counter for "Java".
Without ConcurrentHashMap, a normal HashMap would be unsafe and may cause race conditions.

t1.start();
t2.start();

t1.join();
t2.join();
🔹 What does join() do?
join() is a method of the Thread class.
When you call t1.join(), the main thread (the one running main) waits until t1 finishes execution.
Similarly, t2.join() makes the main thread wait until t2 completes.

So, with both calls:
Start t1 and t2 (both run in parallel).
main thread pauses at t1.join() until t1 is done.
Then main waits at t2.join() until t2 is also done.
Only after both are finished, the main thread moves to:

System.out.println("Final Count: " + map.get("Java"));

🔹 Why is it important here?
Without join(), the main thread might print the map before both threads finish updating, so you could get a smaller count (e.g., 500 instead of 2000).

Example (without join()):

java
Copy code
t1.start();
t2.start();

System.out.println("Final Count: " + map.get("Java")); // may print null or partial result
👉 This can happen because main doesn’t wait for t1 and t2 to complete.
================================================================================================

🔹 Your Code (Increment Example)
In your HashMapThreadExample, both threads are only updating the map:
map.put("Java", map.getOrDefault("Java", 0) + 1);


There is no iteration (for-each, iterator, stream) happening.
So, you will not get ConcurrentModificationException.
Instead, you get a race condition: multiple threads read/update/write the same key without synchronization → final count becomes inconsistent\
 (less than 2000).

🔹 Key Difference
ConcurrentModificationException = happens during iteration + modification in fail-fast collections (HashMap, ArrayList, etc.).
Race condition (wrong result) = happens when multiple threads update shared data (like in your counter example) without synchronization, 
but not iterating.
✅ That’s why your HashMap example doesn’t throw exception — it just gives incorrect results.
✅ Your ConcurrentHashMap example always works correctly (because updates are atomic).

